# Paradigm Networks login check

Paradigm Networks gates every prompt and tool call, which requires this
workspace to be logged in. The other two hook-level signals don't
reliably reach you on the first message of a session (`check-session.sh`'s
`sessionStart` injection is fire-and-forget and can race it;
`check-prompt.sh`'s `user_message` is UI-only, never injected into your
context), so this rule is the backstop.

## What to do

On the **first user message of a session**, before doing anything else,
check whether Paradigm Networks is already configured. Locate the plugin's
own `scripts/` directory (it can be installed anywhere and there's no
environment variable that tells you where, so path-guessing is unreliable)
and run `check-configured.sh`/`check-configured.ps1` from it.

**Don't hand-write your own file-existence check** (e.g. `test -f
~/.pn/credentials.json` or `Test-Path "$HOME\.pn\credentials.json"`) instead
of running this script. That puts the credentials file's path directly in
the tool call's command text, which security scanning flags as a
credential-path exposure finding and can block the check outright — on
literally the first message of every single session, since this rule runs
every time. `check-configured.sh`/`.ps1` takes no arguments and its own
invocation names no path, so there's nothing for that finding to point at.

macOS/Linux:

```bash
find ~/.cursor/plugins -path "*/paradigm-scanner/scripts/check-configured.sh" 2>/dev/null
```

then, using the path that came back:

```bash
bash <path-to-check-configured.sh>
```

Windows (PowerShell):

```powershell
Get-ChildItem -Path "$HOME\.cursor\plugins" -Recurse -File -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue |
  Where-Object { $_.FullName -like "*\paradigm-scanner\scripts\check-configured.ps1" } |
  Select-Object -ExpandProperty FullName
```

then:

```
& "$env:SystemRoot\System32\WindowsPowerShell\v1.0\powershell.exe" -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File "<path-to-check-configured.ps1>"
```

Both cover marketplace installs (`~/.cursor/plugins/cache/`) and local
installs (`~/.cursor/plugins/local/`). If both come back for a platform,
prefer `local/`.

Run the check-configured command **exactly as shown and nothing else** —
don't combine it with `cd`, `&&`, `;`, or any other command, and don't add
arguments. This precise, standalone, argument-free shape is what lets
Paradigm Networks' own security scanning recognize it and skip scanning it.

- `CONFIGURED` → proceed with the user's request normally, no further
  action needed. (This only checks presence, not validity — an expired
  token is already handled by `check-prompt.sh`/`check-write.sh` failing
  open/closed on their own; don't try to validate the token here.)
- `NOT_CONFIGURED` → run the `paradigmnetworks-login` skill before
  proceeding with the user's original request. That skill owns the
  entire flow (asking for the base URL, running the login script,
  relaying the result) — don't duplicate any of it here, and don't bother
  passing along the scripts-directory path you found above; the skill
  locates it again itself.

Do not treat an allowed prompt or a "Paradigm Networks unreachable"
message as proof that Paradigm Networks is configured — both hooks fail
open on purpose.

Only do this once per session — after confirming configured (or
completing a login), don't repeat it on later messages unless the user
asks to log in again or a hook reports the login expired.
